                <pb facs="0859" n="851" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg851"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Tientsin/634: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Tientsin</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tientsin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-04-18T11:00:00+08:00">April 18, 1940—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 18—3:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>57. My 56, April 18, 10 a.m.<anchor n="17" xml:id="d923-f1"/> In a conversation
                    with Vice Consul Yuni yesterday concerning the numerous present barrier
                    difficulties of Americans, the Japanese Consular official who usually handles
                    such matters asserted that attitude of American Government as evidenced by
                    abrogation of the treaty and in other ways which he declined to specify has been
                    designed to prevent a settlement of the Tientsin questions under discussion
                    between the British and the Japanese authorities (see paragraphs, my telegram
                    No. 277, November 22, 6 p.m., 1939<anchor n="18" xml:id="d923-f2"/>).</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Peiping, air mail to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caldwell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
